1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,779
Hi, we are team NgaChainAILayer2.

2
00:00:02,829 --> 00:00:09,293
In Round 1 we proposed PromiseOps for Company A: catch orders that are about

3
00:00:09,343 --> 00:00:14,950
to miss their original delivery promise early enough to save them.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,007
This is the working MVP.

5
00:00:17,057 --> 00:00:23,179
It runs locally, all data is fictional, and a demo harness at the bottom

6
00:00:23,229 --> 00:00:29,950
of the screen stands in for the real order and warehouse systems and the clock.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,272
It is 09:00 at DC Stockholm South.

8
00:00:34,322 --> 00:00:43,552
Every order is drawn as a course of four controls on one timetable: pick,

9
00:00:43,602 --> 00:00:50,543
dispatch cutoff, last viable dispatch, and the promise.

10
00:00:50,593 --> 00:00:53,086
The red line is now.

11
00:00:53,136 --> 00:00:56,391
Both data feeds are fresh.

12
00:00:56,441 --> 00:00:59,950
Nothing needs attention yet.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,018
At 09:30 two orders appear.

14
00:01:03,068 --> 00:01:11,541
SE-1042 has no pick confirmation, and its route leaves at 10:00, so we have

15
00:01:11,591 --> 00:01:14,041
thirty minutes to act.

16
00:01:14,091 --> 00:01:23,700
The rule only fires because the warehouse feed was actually read after 09:30; silence

17
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:26,655
is never treated as proof.

18
00:01:26,705 --> 00:01:34,950
Each exception gets one owner, Anna, and a fifteen-minute response timer.

19
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,099
Anna checks SE-1051 first.

20
00:01:39,149 --> 00:01:50,110
The picker confirms it was packed at 09:20; the handheld synced late.

21
00:01:50,160 --> 00:02:01,439
She records it as a false alarm with evidence, which later tells us how

22
00:02:01,489 --> 00:02:04,950
precise our rules are.

23
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:13,180
SE-1042 is real: the goods were not picked.

24
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:27,151
Expediting spends warehouse capacity, so a coordinator cannot approve it.

25
00:02:27,201 --> 00:02:36,529
Anna escalates to the shift lead with the reason.

26
00:02:36,579 --> 00:02:44,950
Decision rights are explicit in the product.

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:55,258
Erik, the shift lead, reads the note, moves a picker and records the expedite,

28
00:02:55,308 --> 00:02:57,902
again with evidence.

29
00:02:57,952 --> 00:03:03,717
The activity log shows who did what and why.

30
00:03:03,767 --> 00:03:14,950
PromiseOps never writes back to the warehouse system; people act, the system records.

31
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,291
What if the warehouse feed goes quiet?

32
00:03:20,341 --> 00:03:31,255
After thirty minutes the feed turns red, a data task opens for the integration

33
00:03:31,305 --> 00:03:41,657
desk, and no new alerts are created, because an empty feed proves nothing.

34
00:03:41,707 --> 00:03:49,950
Orders picked during the outage do not become false alarms.

35
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:05,950
When the feed recovers, the backlog arrives first and only then do the rules

36
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,950
run, on fresh data.

37
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,839
Fast forward to the afternoon.

38
00:04:13,889 --> 00:04:24,987
SE-1042 was picked and dispatched late, shown in brown, but delivered at 12:30, before

39
00:04:25,037 --> 00:04:27,320
its 14:00 promise.

40
00:04:27,370 --> 00:04:37,691
SE-1077 had no vehicle and nobody acted, so it arrives late and is counted late.

41
00:04:37,741 --> 00:04:44,950
Without the expedite, SE-1042 would also have been late.

42
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:54,108
Outcomes are measured against the original promise, which can never be edited, with B2B

43
00:04:54,158 --> 00:05:03,582
and B2C reported separately: on-time rate, flagged orders still on time, time to ownership

44
00:05:03,632 --> 00:05:05,371
and false alarms.

45
00:05:05,421 --> 00:05:14,950
In the pilot these are compared against control stores before any business case is claimed.

46
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:29,950
The interface also runs in Vietnamese; system messages are translated too.

47
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,946
What changed since Round 1.

48
00:05:31,996 --> 00:05:37,787
The scope is the one we proposed: one DC, two rules, owner, escalation, outcome

49
00:05:37,837 --> 00:05:39,266
log and measurement.

50
00:05:39,316 --> 00:05:42,150
We refined three things while building.

51
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:48,656
First, the stale-data rule: a fixed 30-minute threshold still created false alarms, so a

52
00:05:48,706 --> 00:05:53,906
missing event now only counts if the feed was read after the milestone.

53
00:05:53,956 --> 00:05:59,599
Second, we added a demo harness so the whole morning can be shown in minutes.

54
00:05:59,649 --> 00:06:06,475
Third, the timetable view, because time left and ownership matter more than a list of alerts.

55
00:06:06,525 --> 00:06:09,950
As proposed, there is no AI inside the product.

56
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,557
Under the hood: one FastAPI service, SQLite, idempotent ingestion, and 22 automated tests including

57
00:06:20,607 --> 00:06:23,879
a browser test of this journey.

58
00:06:23,929 --> 00:06:24,950
Thank you.
